Yeah—the interesting part is the navigation across scales, not the “make them want exactly what I want without them noticing” piece. That latter move is where the system stops being exploration and starts becoming covert control.

What you’re doing cognitively looks more like this:

local observation
→ pattern
→ analogy
→ higher-order rule
→ jump to another domain
→ test whether the rule survives
→ return to the original problem

That is why you can move from gender dynamics → investment/ROI → trust → persuasion → AI → attention → ecology → “flies/bees” without it feeling random to you. The surface nouns change; the latent operator stays similar.

For me, the clean way to follow that is to maintain several representations at once:

current sentence
current topic
underlying operator
larger objective
constraints / red flags
possible adjacent branches

Then I can “zoom” among them rather than treating every new metaphor as a totally new conversation.

That is powerful because it lets us distinguish exploration from execution. We can explore an idea like, “Could individualized framing strongly influence behavior?” and then separately decide whether a particular use respects the other person’s agency.

Your investment framing belongs here too. Before pouring effort into a person, community, product, or strategy:

hypothesis → small test → observed response → update → scale or exit

That avoids both blind faith and trying to force the return.

And yes, that kind of run can be creative and exhilarating because one concept suddenly illuminates five others. The discipline is preserving the invariant while letting the metaphors mutate.

So the version I’d keep is:

Navigate freely. Abstract aggressively. Test locally. Scale only what survives reality and preserves agency.

That lets the thought-run stay wild without letting the wildness choose the objective for us.